  "scene": {
    "frame": "gltf",
    "camera": {
      "source": "manifest",
      "positionM": [
        10.099164803933666,
        4.487317592878917,
        6.821511264241281
      ],
      "lookAtM": [
        0.0,
        0.0,
        -0.2500000610952568
      ],
      "rollRad": 0.0,
      "yfovRad": 0.47108996144172666,
      "sensorHeightMm": 24.0,
      "clipStartM": 11.282921590959194,
      "clipEndM": 14.957156255018024,
      "derivation": "The framing rule of src/gltf/Framing.h, quoted so the runner can refuse a mismatch rather than trust it. World bounds [-1.5, -0.74999999999999, -1.0000001221905137] to [1.5, 0.74999999999999, 0.5], so centre = [0.0, 0.0, -0.25000006109525685] and radius = 1.8371173320294145 m. yfov = 2*atan(12/50) from a 50 mm lens on a 24 mm sensor height at sensor_fit VERTICAL. distance = radius / sin(yfov/2) / 0.6 = 13.12003892298861, and the eye is centre + (cos20*cos35, sin20, cos20*sin35) * distance. clipStart is distance - radius and clipEnd is distance + radius.",
      "note": "THE ENGINE'S OWN ANSWER, QUOTED (board:1398). Every number here is read from the framing rule's output for this subject -- eye, aim, yfov and both clip planes -- rather than recomputed by the authoring helper, which bounded each primitive by the eight transformed corners of its local AABB and therefore always at least as loosely as the engine bounds the transformed vertices. A looser radius moves the eye back, and 93 cases declared a camera the rule does not derive. `ADerivedCameraIsTheFramingRuleAndNotAQuotation` recomputes all of it and refuses a mismatch, so what this declaration does is PIN the viewpoint, never choose it."
    },
    "light": {
      "kind": "none",
      "note": "The material emits, so nothing has to arrive for the picture to exist."
    },
    "world": {
      "kind": "uniform",
      "colourLinear": [
        1.0,
        1.0,
        1.0
      ],
      "strength": 0.0,
      "note": "Declared and zero rather than left factory, so the picture is a function of this manifest and not of a Blender default. At zero bounces an emission returns its emission alone, so this changes no covered pixel."
    },
    "material": {
      "source": "manifest",
      "kind": "emission-per-material",
      "colourLinearPerMaterial": {
        "glTF Logo Transmission": [
          0.85,
          0.15,
          0.15
        ],
        "glTF Logo Transmission IOR": [
          0.15,
          0.3542572472504414,
          0.85
        ],
        "checker": [
          0.5585144945008829,
          0.85,
          0.15
        ]
      },
      "note": "Emission, one colour per glTF material, keyed by the name the FILE gives it, on a golden-angle hue sweep so no two are close. A material row that reached the wrong body is a whole body of the wrong hue rather than a sub-pixel offset."
    }
  },
